    h1: "Do I Need a PE Stamp for a Rooftop Solar Installation?",
    answer: "In most jurisdictions, yes — a rooftop solar installation needs a PE-stamped structural review before the permit issues. The panels add dead load to the roof, they change the wind behavior of the roof surface, and the attachment or ballast system has to keep the array on the roof in a design wind event. The PE's review verifies the existing roof structure can carry the added weight, designs or verifies the mounting and its connections, and checks wind uplift on the array. For Florida's hurricane zones, that wind check is the heart of the review; everywhere else, it's still the item plan check reads first.\n\nWhat I verify starts with the existing roof: the framing system, member sizes and spans, and the condition — because a structural review is only as good as the structure it's reviewing, and older roofs with undocumented modifications need field verification before I rely on the drawings. Then the array: panel weights, the mounting hardware, and whether the system is attached through the roof or ballasted. Attached systems need the connection capacity into the actual framing verified, with flashing and waterproofing details that don't compromise the roof. Ballasted systems need the added weight checked against the roof capacity and the wind design proving the ballast keeps the array in place without relying on friction alone.\n\nThe wind analysis is where solar reviews live or die. Panels near roof edges and corners see dramatically higher uplift pressures than panels in the field of the roof, and the mounting layout has to respect those zones. I check the wind pressures used, the zone layout of the array, and the capacity of every attachment or the adequacy of the ballast in each zone. When the existing structure verifies, the mounting verifies, and the wind design holds in every zone, the PE seals the solar structural set.",
    directAnswer: "Most jurisdictions require a PE-stamped structural review for rooftop solar, verifying the existing roof can carry the added load, the mounting or ballast system is adequate, and the array resists wind uplift in every roof zone — with Florida's hurricane wind checks being the most demanding.",
