        heading: "Verifying the existing roof",
        body: "Every rooftop solar review stands on the existing structure assessment. I start with the available structural drawings and work forward: what is the framing system, what are the member sizes and spans, what was the original design load, and how much capacity remains for the solar addition? The array's distributed weight is usually modest, but concentrated loads at attachment points and the ballast weight of unattached systems can govern locally. Where drawings are missing or the building's history suggests modifications, field verification fills the gap — measuring members, confirming spans, and checking condition, because deterioration or prior alterations change the capacity math.\n\nRoof condition matters beyond structure. The review notes the roofing membrane's age and state, because a solar array with a 25-year life on a roof with five years left is a planning failure the PE should flag even though it's not strictly structural. Drainage matters too — ballasted systems and rail layouts that pond water create problems no structural calculation fixes. A good solar structural review sees the roof as a system, not just a platform.",
