    h1: "Does an ADU Need a PE Stamp?",
    answer: "An ADU needs a PE stamp wherever the AHJ requires engineered structural or energy documents — which covers most ADUs beyond the simplest single-story conventional builds. California's ADU statutes streamlined the planning side dramatically, but they didn't waive engineering: two-story ADUs, units on hillsides or in high-seismic zones, garage conversions with new structural work, and any ADU with non-conventional framing still need a licensed engineer's review and seal. The stamp covers what the prescriptive conventional-construction provisions don't.\n\nWhat I review on an ADU is the full small-building package. Structurally: the foundation design for the site's soils — expansive clay in Texas ADUs, seismic detailing in California — the gravity framing, and the lateral system with its holdowns and shear walls detailed for the actual geometry. Small buildings fail the lateral check more often than people expect, because compact plans with lots of openings leave little room for shear walls. Energy: California ADUs need Title 24 compliance documentation, and the PE's review reconciles the energy forms with the actual design — envelope, HVAC, water heating — before sealing.\n\nThe ADU-specific wrinkle is utilities and separation. Detached ADUs need their own electrical service or a verified feeder from the main house, plumbing connections to the site's sewer or septic with capacity confirmed, and fire separation where the code requires it for the proximity to the primary dwelling and property lines. In Arizona and Texas, where ADU ordinances are newer and vary widely by city, I confirm the local AHJ's submittal expectations early — the engineering is consistent, but what the counter asks for at intake isn't. When the structure verifies, the energy complies, and the utilities are resolved, the PE seals and the ADU permits.",
