    h1: "Does a Tenant Improvement Need a PE Stamp?",
    answer: "A tenant improvement needs a PE stamp whenever the work goes beyond cosmetic finishes into engineered territory: cutting new openings in walls, adding structural loads, modifying the HVAC, plumbing, or electrical systems, or changing egress and occupancy. Paint and carpet don't need engineering; a new storefront opening, a commercial kitchen in a former retail space, or a dental office's added plumbing and electrical very much do. The AHJ decides the threshold, but the practical rule is that any TI touching structure or MEP systems needs a licensed engineer's review and seal.\n\nWhat I review in a TI set starts with what's existing. The base building's structural system, the available capacity in the electrical service and panels, the HVAC system's spare capacity, and the plumbing risers available to the space — the TI design has to live within the building's realities. New openings in bearing walls or shear walls get structural analysis and detailing; I verify the header or frame design and, critically in seismic country, that the opening doesn't compromise the lateral system. MEP changes get the same rigor as new construction: load calculations for added HVAC and electrical, plumbing isometrics for new fixtures, all coordinated with the base building systems.\n\nThe TI wrinkle that surprises tenants is the base building's constraints. The landlord's building has a finite electrical service, a finite HVAC capacity, and structural systems designed for the original layout — and the TI can't exceed any of them without upgrades that need the landlord's approval and sometimes the AHJ's. I verify the TI design against the base building's documented capacities before sealing, because a TI set that's perfect on paper but impossible in the building is a permit that dies at plan check or, worse, in construction.",
