        body: "TI structural work is surgery on someone else's building, and the review treats it that way. I start by establishing what exists: the structural system, which walls are bearing, where the lateral elements are, and what the floor structure can carry. Proposed openings get analyzed for their effect on gravity and lateral behavior — a new storefront in a shear wall line is a lateral redesign, not a header schedule. New loads get checked against the existing structure's capacity: the rooftop unit on the TI's new HVAC, the file room's floor loading, the equipment platform. Every structural modification gets designed, detailed, and sealed as its own engineered scope within the TI set.\n\nIn seismic jurisdictions — California TIs especially — the lateral check is the heart of the structural review. Openings in shear walls, removed wall segments, and added mass all change the building's seismic behavior, and the code's existing-building provisions govern what's permitted. I verify the TI's lateral design against those provisions, detail the connections and holdowns the modification requires, and confirm the work doesn't trigger a broader seismic retrofit the owner didn't budget for. That last check — scope containment — is the TI structural review's most valuable deliverable.",
