    h1: "Does a Change of Occupancy Need PE Review?",
    answer: "A change of occupancy needs PE review whenever the new use changes what the code demands of the building — which is most of the time. Occupancy classification drives egress requirements, fire protection, allowable structural loads, plumbing fixture counts, and ventilation rates. Moving from office to restaurant, warehouse to gym, or retail to daycare each rewrites the code analysis, and the AHJ won't approve the new use until a licensed design professional demonstrates the building meets the new occupancy's requirements. The PE's review is that demonstration, sealed.\n\nWhat I do is a code analysis of the new occupancy against the existing building. Egress first: occupant load for the new use, exit widths, travel distances, and whether the existing exits serve the new occupant count — restaurants and assembly uses routinely break the egress math of the office or retail space they replace. Fire protection: sprinkler and alarm requirements for the new occupancy, and whether the existing systems comply or need upgrades. Structural: the new use's design loads against what the structure was built for — a warehouse floor designed for storage handles a gym's loads differently than a retail floor handles restaurant kitchen equipment.\n\nMEP follows the occupancy change. Plumbing fixture counts are occupancy-driven, and the jump from office to restaurant or daycare usually means more fixtures, sometimes a larger water service, and grease waste where food service enters. HVAC ventilation rates follow the occupancy's air requirements, and the electrical load analysis reflects the new equipment. The PE's sealed package ties it all together: the code analysis showing what the new occupancy requires, the evaluation of the existing building against it, and the engineered upgrades closing the gaps. That package is what the AHJ reviews for the occupancy change.",
